<h2>The suit and its fortunes below</h2>
<p>The Wakf Board sued for a permanent injunction restraining interference with its possession of mafi inam lands adjoining a mosque, dargah, graveyard and well — properties notified as wakf. The trial court decreed the suit, resting on the 1968 survey report, the 1989 gazette notification and pahanis of 1956-59 recording the land as maafi inam held in service of the institutions. The High Court reversed, reasoning that the Board had produced no occupancy certificate under the Telangana inams abolition law and that the revenue record did not establish wakf character or possession.</p>
<p>The defendants' claim traced to pattadar passbooks of their vendors and an agreement of sale coupled with a general power of attorney — instruments of the kind that populate much contested land litigation.</p>

<h2>The Supreme Court's analysis</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Framing: injunction or declaration?</strong> Applying Anathula Sudhakar, the Court asked whether the defendants' assertions raised a genuine cloud on title. It held in terms that there was no cloud over the title of the suit scheduled property, so the bare injunction suit was maintainable.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>The vendors had nothing to sell.</strong> The land was a service inam, held for maintaining the mosque, dargah and graveyard. Holders in that capacity could not convey it; the purchasers therefore acquired no competing title.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Finality under Section 6.</strong> Disputes to the published wakf list had to be raised within one year of publication under the Wakf Act, 1954. None was, and no later-knowledge case was pleaded; the listing stood conclusive.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Result.</strong> The Court found absolutely no reason to uphold the High Court's order, set it aside, and made the trial court's injunction absolute. The connected contempt petitions were dismissed on their own facts.</div>
</div>
<div class="note">
<p>The judgment distinguished the possession-centric decision in Balkrishna Dattatraya Galande, underlining that each injunction suit turns on whether possession and title are genuinely in doubt — not on a mechanical rule that every denial of title forces a declaratory suit.</p>
</div>

<h2>Why the decision matters beyond wakf law</h2>
<p>Two features give the ruling general importance for property practice. The first is its disciplined application of the Anathula Sudhakar framework: courts should not drive a possessing plaintiff to a declaratory suit — with its heavier court fees and longer trial — merely because the defendant produces paper. The quality of the rival claim decides whether a cloud exists, and purchases from persons who held land in a fiduciary or service capacity generate no cloud at all.</p>
<p>The second is the reminder that statutory limitation regimes attached to published lists and notifications — under wakf legislation, land ceiling laws, consolidation schemes — are not decorative. Decades of silence after publication forecloses the challenge, whatever the strength of the claimant's later-acquired documents. Due diligence in land transactions must therefore reach beyond the revenue record to gazettes and statutory lists, where a single unchallenged entry can be dispositive.</p>

<h2>Practical pointers</h2>
<div class="check">
<p>Choose the frame of a possession suit deliberately: plead possession with particulars, and seek declaration only where the opponent's claim raises a genuine triable doubt about title.</p>
<p>In transactions involving inam, endowment or institutional lands, verify the capacity in which the vendor holds — service holders and managers cannot convey the corpus.</p>
<p>Search gazette notifications and statutory lists (wakf, endowment, ceiling) during title diligence; limitation under those statutes may already have closed the question.</p>
<p>Interim status quo orders protect the character of property, not every activity on it; contempt lies for alterations of substance, not for grievances of a party without subsisting interest.</p>
</div>
